WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.640
Avengers: Endgame is filled with mindblowing
moments on a scale never before seen in cinematic

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:05.640
history.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.720
But it also includes a lot of small, quiet
moments, and hidden details including one

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.180
super important cameo most fans missed.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.440
"He's coming!"

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.820
"... who?"

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.180
It happens during the support group scene
early in the film, where Steve Rogers is shown

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.210
discussing the impact of the snap with several
other survivors, one of whom is played by

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.160
Endgame co-director Joe Russo.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.020
But while some fans of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe were geeking out about that, they

00:00:34.021 --> 00:00:38.000
may not have noticed that one of the other
support group members is none other than legendary

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.910
comic book writer and artist Jim Starlin,
the man who created Thanos, Gamora, and Drax

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:43.910
in the first place.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:48.240
Credited simply as "Support group Man #1,"
Starlin opened up about his Endgame cameo

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.440
in a post on Instagram.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:14.240
Though Starlin's cameo was small, his involvement
in the film at all is a big deal considering

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.409
some of the major issues he has had with Marvel
in the recent past.

00:01:17.409 --> 00:01:21.999
In January 2017, for instance, Starlin posted
on Facebook that his royalty check for his

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.620
minor villain KGBeast appearing in Batman
v Superman was,

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:37.800
And in December, 2017, Starlin publicly parted
ways with the comic book branch of Marvel

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:42.270
as he felt editorial decisions had severely
undermined a long running Thanos project he

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.829
had been working on for the company.

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:47.630
But it seems as though the mutual admiration
between Starlin and brothers Joe and Anthony

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.369
Russo has smoothed over those past difficulties.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:55.409
In February, 2017, Starlin posted on Facebook
that after seeing his Infinity Gauntlet tome

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:59.329
in the Infinity War behind the scenes short,
he was excited to see what the directors might

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:00.740
do with the material, adding,

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.000
To which the Russos replied,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.380
It's almost as if appearing in a movie with
his most popular creation was Starlin's destiny.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.960
"Dread it, run from it, destiny sarrives all
the same.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.420
And now it's here."

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:26.040
Though Starlin didn't appear in Avengers:
Infinity War, he told his Facebook fans that

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.320
he was "proud" of the movie.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.620
And he told SyFy that he was particularly
happy with Avengers: Endgame.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.120
He also doesn't think that we've seen the
last of Thanos, regardless of how Endgame

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:51.860
concluded.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:09.719
So, there you have it folks: the one tiny
cameo in Avengers: Endgame that slipped right

00:03:09.719 --> 00:03:14.420
under your radar has serious ties to Marvel
and if Starlin has any sway in the decision-making

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.069
process, Thanos might return from space dust
for another Marvel movie.

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:21.880
Let's hope if that happens, the heroes know
to aim for his head the first time around.

